 ## Overview
-The Extended Tandem Algorithm is a parallel, bloc-wise extension of the Tandem Algorithm [3675-tandem-algorithm.md] designed to extract and simplify isosurfaces from very large volumetric datasets while minimizing memory consumption. It solves the scalability bottleneck of the sequential Tandem Algorithm, where queue updates and simplification steps become too memory-intensive for high-resolution data (e.g., generating millions of triangles per layer) [S1, S2]. Unlike the parent method, which processes the entire volume in a single pass, this method splits the dataset into blocks, distributes them across computing nodes, and employs a component dumping mechanism to persist finalized surface components to disk [S1, S2]. This allows the extraction process to continue on sub-parts of the dataset without losing previously computed geometry, making it suitable for geophysical datasets with numerous small, disconnected components [S1, S2].
+The Extended Tandem Algorithm is a parallel, bloc-wise extension of the Tandem Algorithm [3675-tandem-algorithm.md] designed to extract and simplify isosurfaces from very large volumetric datasets while minimizing memory consumption [S1, S2]. It solves the scalability bottleneck of the sequential Tandem Algorithm, where queue updates and simplification steps become too memory-intensive for high-resolution data, such as generating 1,250,000 triangles per layer on a $1626 \times 7028 \times 2000$ dataset [S1, S2]. Unlike the parent method, which processes the entire volume in a single pass, this method splits the dataset into blocks, distributes them across computing nodes, and employs a component dumping mechanism to persist finalized surface components to disk [S1, S2]. This allows the extraction process to continue on sub-parts of the dataset without losing previously computed geometry, making it suitable for geophysical datasets with numerous small, disconnected components [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The algorithm operates using a worker-manager architecture. The dataset is split into blocks and distributed to computing nodes (workers). Each worker extracts and semi-simplifies a local isosurface, then merges it with adjacent semi-simplified isosurfaces received from other workers. The core simplification logic relies on the Tandem Algorithm's edge collapse strategy with a "time lag" mechanism to prevent artifacts near the advancing front [S1, S2].
+The algorithm operates using a worker-manager architecture [S2]. The dataset is split into blocks and distributed to computing nodes (workers) [S2]. Each worker extracts and semi-simplifies a local isosurface, then merges it with adjacent semi-simplified isosurfaces received from other workers [S2]. The core simplification logic relies on the Tandem Algorithm's edge collapse strategy with a "time lag" mechanism to prevent artifacts near the advancing front [S1, S2].
 
